[FINAL-TEXT] Enough evidence. The `minrtt: 0.291ms` from `ss` proves intrinsic link latency is sub-ms. Yet iperf consistently hits ~113 Mbps with TCP retransmits (57-88 per 5s test) — BOTH on end-to-end AND on the direct router1→router2 segment. No internal TC, no iptables, no errors on any interface. The retransmits happen precisely when TCP CWND overshoots the rate cap and packets get tail-dropped. The cap is invisible from inside the containers (only pfifo_fast inside, no tbf/netem) → externally applied bandwidth limit on the Kathara link.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1, router2
diagnosis_text:
The inter-router link between router1:eth0 and router2:eth0 has been artificially rate-limited to approximately 100 Mbps by an externally-applied Kathara link-rate parameter (a Linux TC token-bucket-filter placed on the host-side veth pair). This is invisible from within the containers — all internal qdiscs on every device show unmodified pfifo_fast with zero drops or overlimits — but its effect is unmistakable in measurement: iperf3 from router1 directly to router2 (no host forwarding involved) achieves only ~113 Mbps with 57 TCP retransmits in five seconds, and end-to-end (pc1→pc2) yields ~128 Mbps with 88 retransmits over the same window. The BGP session itself reports an estimated RTT of 8-9 ms on a link whose kernel-measured minrtt is 0.291 ms, consistent with delayed-ACK inflation on a throttled pipe. When TCP's congestion window grows past what the rate cap can drain, the token bucket silently drops the excess packets; TCP then retransmits, CWND halves, and throughput oscillates — producing exactly the "wading through treacle" application experience the user describes. BGP configuration, routing tables, iptables, MTU, and all host configurations are correct and not contributing to the fault. The root cause is a Kathara lab.conf `rate` setting on the router1↔router2 link that caps it at ~100 Mbps. The fix is to remove or raise the rate parameter in lab.conf for that link (e.g., delete `link_rate` or set it to the desired line rate), then restart the affected Kathara lab so the host-side TC rule is removed.
=== END ===